<article class="content editorial-guide">
 <p class="eyebrow">HIJRI CALENDAR GUIDE</p><h1>How the Hijri calendar works</h1>
 <p>The Islamic (Hijri) calendar follows lunar months rather than the Gregorian solar year. It helps Muslims keep track of Ramadan, Eid, Hajj, the White Days and other important dates. A calculated date is a useful reference, but it is not necessarily the local religious announcement.</p>
 <nav class="guide-quick-links" aria-label="Related Hijri tools"><a href="/tools/hijri-converter/">Check Hijri date today ↗</a><a href="/tools/islamic-events-calendar/">Islamic events ↗</a><a href="/tools/printable-hijri-calendar/">Printable calendar ↗</a><a href="/tools/white-days-calendar/">White Days ↗</a></nav>
 <h2>Hijri and Gregorian dates are different</h2><p>A lunar year generally has 354 or 355 days, which is about 11 days shorter than a Gregorian year. The same Islamic month therefore falls at different points in the Gregorian calendar over time. An ordinary Hijri month contains 29 or 30 days.</p>
 <h2>What is the Umm al-Qura calendar?</h2><p>Our converters use the Umm al-Qura calendar supported by the browser's international date-formatting functions. It provides consistent calculated dates for planning and conversion; it is not a live report of local crescent sightings. Other calculated or observational calendars can give a different result.</p>
 <h2>Why might today's Islamic date differ by one day?</h2><p>Local moon-sighting decisions, the calendar system and the fact that an Islamic date begins at sunset can all affect the answer. An online conversion based on a civil date cannot identify the exact start of a religious day in every location. Confirm Ramadan, Eid and Hajj dates with your recognised local authority.</p>
 <h2>Converting and printing</h2><p>Use the <a href="/tools/hijri-converter/">Hijri Date Today converter</a> to move between Gregorian and Hijri dates. For planning, the <a href="/tools/printable-hijri-calendar/">printable Hijri calendar</a> shows a calculated month, and the <a href="/tools/white-days-calendar/">White Days tool</a> marks the 13th, 14th and 15th with appropriate fasting caveats.</p>
 <h2>Limitations</h2><p>Do not treat a conversion as a fatwa, official new-moon decision or guaranteed local festival date. The device timezone, the moment of sunset and country-specific announcements matter. When a date is important for worship, use the guidance of your trusted community.</p>
